WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.230
Tyler: I'm Tyler, and this is OpenTelemetry Far and Wide.

2
00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:05.790
Nikola: And I'm Nikola.

3
00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:10.629
Nikola: Start with broad coverage, then add the detail your investigation needs.

4
00:00:10.869 --> 00:00:11.745
Tyler: One request.

5
00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:13.059
Tyler: One missing answer.

6
00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:14.373
Tyler: Let's follow it.

7
00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:16.781
Tyler: Checkout is slow.

8
00:00:16.781 --> 00:00:20.930
Tyler: Nikola, I've narrowed the problem down to the service named checkout.

9
00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:21.557
Nikola: Excellent.

10
00:00:21.557 --> 00:00:25.045
Nikola: The service name has really pulled its weight there.

11
00:00:25.045 --> 00:00:26.983
Nikola: What is it actually doing?

12
00:00:27.223 --> 00:00:28.370
Tyler: That's the question.

13
00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:32.574
Tyler: Imagine you own the platform, but different teams own the applications.

14
00:00:32.574 --> 00:00:38.307
Tyler: Our illustrated request enters a Go gateway, passes through Java checkout, and reaches Node inventory.

15
00:00:38.547 --> 00:00:41.021
Nikola: So you can follow the request across services.

16
00:00:41.021 --> 00:00:44.422
Nikola: But the expensive work inside checkout still has no useful name.

17
00:00:44.662 --> 00:00:47.956
Tyler: I could ask every team to change its instrumentation.

18
00:00:47.956 --> 00:00:50.884
Tyler: That sounds like a meeting before an investigation.

19
00:00:51.124 --> 00:00:51.821
Nikola: Start smaller.

20
00:00:51.821 --> 00:00:55.655
Nikola: What can the existing signals answer, and which observation is missing?

21
00:00:55.895 --> 00:00:57.327
Tyler: That's our investigation.

22
00:00:57.327 --> 00:00:59.714
Tyler: Broad coverage gets us oriented.

23
00:00:59.714 --> 00:01:03.534
Tyler: Selected application detail should explain the local work.

24
00:01:03.774 --> 00:01:06.921
Nikola: And adding a span will not magically make checkout faster.

25
00:01:06.921 --> 00:01:09.439
Nikola: We still have to fix the actual application.

26
00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.958
Tyler: Think of a trace as one linked request.

27
00:01:13.958 --> 00:01:16.026
Tyler: Its spans describe timed operations.

28
00:01:16.026 --> 00:01:19.747
Tyler: First, follow that request across gateway, checkout, and inventory.

29
00:01:19.987 --> 00:01:21.051
Nikola: That gives us context.

30
00:01:21.051 --> 00:01:25.037
Nikola: Which service deserves attention, and how does it connect to the rest of the request?

31
00:01:25.277 --> 00:01:26.964
Tyler: Now look inside checkout.

32
00:01:26.964 --> 00:01:31.601
Tyler: A selected application operation can become another span within that request.

33
00:01:31.841 --> 00:01:34.142
Nikola: Same investigation, a more specific observation.

34
00:01:34.142 --> 00:01:38.362
Nikola: We haven't replaced the service view; we've explained something inside it.

35
00:01:38.602 --> 00:01:43.097
Tyler: One qualification: eBPF instrumentation and language agents have overlapping capabilities.

36
00:01:43.097 --> 00:01:49.391
Tyler: This example uses broad coverage first and method detail where the question needs it.

37
00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:55.846
Tyler: Let's establish that broad view through the shared deployment workflow.

38
00:01:55.846 --> 00:02:02.600
Tyler: Our proposed Operator integration should manage eBPF coverage and selected language instrumentation together.

39
00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.534
Nikola: Shared workflow sounds convenient.

40
00:02:04.534 --> 00:02:07.923
Nikola: What does the platform actually have to bring?

41
00:02:08.163 --> 00:02:14.901
Tyler: A working cluster, the applications, admission components where needed, and somewhere to export telemetry.

42
00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:17.788
Tyler: One command can coordinate the work.

43
00:02:17.788 --> 00:02:20.676
Tyler: It cannot make those dependencies disappear.

44
00:02:20.916 --> 00:02:24.922
Nikola: So the command finishing is the beginning of the check, not the end.

45
00:02:25.162 --> 00:02:25.622
Tyler: Right.

46
00:02:25.622 --> 00:02:28.837
Tyler: The person investigating needs useful request evidence.

47
00:02:28.837 --> 00:02:36.648
Tyler: A successful installation message doesn't tell them whether gateway, checkout, and inventory belong to the same trace.

48
00:02:36.888 --> 00:02:39.141
Nikola: Now follow the illustrated request.

49
00:02:39.141 --> 00:02:44.100
Nikola: Gateway reaches checkout, checkout reaches inventory, and their operations are linked.

50
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.256
Nikola: That's the baseline we're trying to establish.

51
00:02:47.496 --> 00:02:53.508
Tyler: I want to check service identities and parent relationships before interpreting any timing.

52
00:02:53.508 --> 00:02:58.596
Tyler: Otherwise a convincing diagram can send us toward the wrong explanation.

53
00:02:58.836 --> 00:03:00.764
Nikola: And these bar lengths are schematic.

54
00:03:00.764 --> 00:03:04.621
Nikola: They aren't measured latency values or a recording of a completed deployment.

55
00:03:04.861 --> 00:03:12.018
Tyler: Once the real request path is trustworthy, we can ask a much better question than, is something somewhere slow?

56
00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:14.884
Nikola: Which local work inside checkout explains its time?

57
00:03:14.884 --> 00:03:17.509
Nikola: Coverage has brought us to the right door.

58
00:03:17.509 --> 00:03:19.479
Nikola: Now we need to look inside.

59
00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:22.760
Tyler: Stay with checkout's baseline span.

60
00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:29.091
Tyler: Inventory is nested inside it, but the downstream call doesn't explain all the local work.

61
00:03:29.331 --> 00:03:33.136
Nikola: Can I add the bars together and report a really impressive total?

62
00:03:33.376 --> 00:03:36.733
Tyler: Only if you want to count some time twice.

63
00:03:36.733 --> 00:03:40.837
Tyler: A parent span can already include the time in its children.

64
00:03:41.077 --> 00:03:41.433
Nikola: Fair.

65
00:03:41.433 --> 00:03:46.780
Nikola: What would everyone want to observe next: another service map, or an operation inside checkout?

66
00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.206
Nikola: Give that five seconds.

67
00:03:53.446 --> 00:03:55.206
Tyler: A local application operation.

68
00:03:55.206 --> 00:03:59.167
Tyler: For this workload, the target is a pricing calculation.

69
00:03:59.407 --> 00:04:01.950
Nikola: And selecting that method takes application knowledge.

70
00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:06.673
Nikola: Someone has to know which class and operation mean something to this investigation.

71
00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:09.543
Tyler: We aren't discovering business meaning automatically.

72
00:04:09.543 --> 00:04:13.488
Tyler: We're choosing the observation that could close this gap.

73
00:04:13.728 --> 00:04:14.108
Nikola: Good.

74
00:04:14.108 --> 00:04:16.007
Nikola: A reason for more detail.

75
00:04:16.007 --> 00:04:19.426
Nikola: Tyler, let's get that calculation into the request view.

76
00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:24.330
Nikola: Before changing anything, say what success would look like.

77
00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:30.464
Nikola: We want a span around the existing pricing calculation, linked inside a fresh checkout request.

78
00:04:30.704 --> 00:04:36.008
Tyler: Same application image, more information about the work it already does?

79
00:04:36.248 --> 00:04:36.709
Nikola: Yes.

80
00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:39.472
Nikola: The running process can still change.

81
00:04:39.472 --> 00:04:45.458
Nikola: With startup agent injection, adding or changing the agent can require replacement pods.

82
00:04:45.458 --> 00:04:48.682
Nikola: No application rebuild doesn't mean no rollout.

83
00:04:48.922 --> 00:04:50.768
Tyler: That's an important distinction.

84
00:04:50.768 --> 00:04:53.997
Tyler: Where does the method selection come from?

85
00:04:54.237 --> 00:04:58.313
Nikola: For this Java example, configuration identifies a class and method.

86
00:04:58.313 --> 00:05:02.796
Nikola: The agent must load in the process and apply that setting.

87
00:05:02.796 --> 00:05:06.057
Nikola: A configuration field alone doesn't prove either happened.

88
00:05:06.297 --> 00:05:11.765
Tyler: So while the profile converges, we keep the original diagnostic question visible.

89
00:05:11.765 --> 00:05:19.056
Tyler: We're waiting for a named operation in a new request, not just a green workload indicator.

90
00:05:19.296 --> 00:05:21.921
Nikola: And keep the baseline request available.

91
00:05:21.921 --> 00:05:28.045
Nikola: Old and new processes can coexist during a transition, and their instrumentation can differ.

92
00:05:28.045 --> 00:05:31.544
Nikola: We need to know which request we're inspecting.

93
00:05:31.784 --> 00:05:37.991
Tyler: There it is in the illustrated depth view: Pricing Engine calculate, inside checkout.

94
00:05:37.991 --> 00:05:41.333
Tyler: Gateway and inventory are still around it.

95
00:05:41.573 --> 00:05:42.972
Nikola: That's the useful change.

96
00:05:42.972 --> 00:05:47.170
Nikola: The local operation has a name and a place in the request.

97
00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:52.417
Nikola: An isolated method span in another trace would leave us doing the detective work ourselves.

98
00:05:52.657 --> 00:05:54.536
Tyler: What would make you trust it?

99
00:05:54.776 --> 00:06:02.243
Nikola: The correct parent chain, the intended producer of checkout request spans, and the baseline signals that should remain.

100
00:06:02.243 --> 00:06:07.220
Nikola: Also, verify that the fresh request really came from the new configuration.

101
00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:10.937
Tyler: So the prize is an explanation we can use.

102
00:06:10.937 --> 00:06:14.799
Tyler: The extra colored bar is just how we're seeing it.

103
00:06:15.039 --> 00:06:15.437
Nikola: Exactly.

104
00:06:15.437 --> 00:06:19.411
Nikola: Hold onto that distinction when we compare the two views.

105
00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:24.690
Nikola: Here's the comparison, with both requests visible together.

106
00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:30.750
Nikola: They're separate executions, so use matching framing and a common time scale.

107
00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:35.715
Tyler: Baseline on the left: checkout does some unnamed local work.

108
00:06:35.715 --> 00:06:40.913
Tyler: Depth on the right: a pricing calculation is linked inside checkout.

109
00:06:41.153 --> 00:06:47.282
Nikola: Now we can form a specific hypothesis and decide what to inspect in the application.

110
00:06:47.282 --> 00:06:49.326
Nikola: That is the diagnostic improvement.

111
00:06:49.566 --> 00:06:51.661
Tyler: The tracing got better.

112
00:06:51.661 --> 00:06:53.757
Tyler: Checkout didn't get faster.

113
00:06:53.757 --> 00:06:58.472
Tyler: We have upgraded our explanation, not our pricing algorithm.

114
00:06:58.712 --> 00:07:00.951
Nikola: And that is still a useful result.

115
00:07:00.951 --> 00:07:06.390
Nikola: We can act on a named operation instead of treating the whole service as one mystery interval.

116
00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:07.729
Tyler: All right.

117
00:07:07.729 --> 00:07:09.928
Tyler: The investigation makes sense.

118
00:07:09.928 --> 00:07:19.272
Tyler: How do we operate these two instrumentation paths without creating another hand-maintained project for every application team?

119
00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:24.073
Nikola: Separate the control of instrumentation from the path its telemetry takes.

120
00:07:24.073 --> 00:07:28.118
Nikola: At the top is the proposed Operator integration and its shared deployment workflow.

121
00:07:28.358 --> 00:07:31.828
Tyler: What is that box responsible for, in concrete terms?

122
00:07:32.068 --> 00:07:39.053
Nikola: Selecting an enrolled workload, expressing its intended instrumentation configuration, and coordinating the changes needed to reach it.

123
00:07:39.053 --> 00:07:46.037
Nikola: That coordination is part of the proposed integration, not a finished upstream feature we can assume exists.

124
00:07:46.277 --> 00:07:48.771
Tyler: Underneath it, there are two paths.

125
00:07:48.771 --> 00:07:50.019
Tyler: One configures OBI.

126
00:07:50.019 --> 00:07:53.344
Tyler: The other selects and injects a language agent.

127
00:07:53.344 --> 00:07:57.917
Tyler: eBPF isn't secretly installing the Java agent on its way past.

128
00:07:58.157 --> 00:07:58.611
Nikola: Correct.

129
00:07:58.611 --> 00:07:59.971
Nikola: They're parallel responsibilities.

130
00:07:59.971 --> 00:08:07.681
Nikola: An Operator Instrumentation resource can describe agent settings, and injection annotations select it in the current proof.

131
00:08:07.681 --> 00:08:10.402
Nikola: Custom orchestration handles that proof's transitions.

132
00:08:10.642 --> 00:08:18.054
Tyler: One entry point still needs configuration ownership, explicit workload selection, and checks on the actual running result.

133
00:08:18.054 --> 00:08:22.414
Tyler: We haven't made those responsibilities disappear behind a friendlier button.

134
00:08:22.654 --> 00:08:24.889
Nikola: Now follow the telemetry toward the Collector.

135
00:08:24.889 --> 00:08:31.275
Nikola: Both paths can export there, but they should not both produce the same checkout request spans without an ownership plan.

136
00:08:31.515 --> 00:08:34.499
Tyler: So who owns checkout tracing in this example?

137
00:08:34.739 --> 00:08:36.679
Nikola: In the intended breadth view, OBI.

138
00:08:36.679 --> 00:08:42.499
Nikola: In the depth view, the Java agent supplies checkout request spans while the configured OBI baseline metrics remain.

139
00:08:42.499 --> 00:08:46.056
Nikola: That ownership rule has to be verified during the transition too.

140
00:08:46.296 --> 00:08:51.859
Tyler: Otherwise we could add detail and also add a confusing duplicate request view.

141
00:08:51.859 --> 00:08:53.571
Tyler: More data, less understanding.

142
00:08:53.811 --> 00:08:59.067
Nikola: The operating goal is to choose the workload and profile, then verify what's running and what's exported.

143
00:08:59.067 --> 00:09:02.159
Nikola: A shared workflow should make that easier to do consistently.

144
00:09:02.399 --> 00:09:04.361
Tyler: Let's test the lifecycle, then.

145
00:09:04.361 --> 00:09:09.463
Tyler: If we own the configuration centrally, can we also manage an instrumentation upgrade?

146
00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:13.733
Nikola: An upgrade has a requested state and an observed state.

147
00:09:13.733 --> 00:09:16.199
Nikola: Keep both in view before and after convergence.

148
00:09:16.439 --> 00:09:18.122
Tyler: I've changed the version field.

149
00:09:18.122 --> 00:09:20.477
Tyler: Can I put upgraded on the slide?

150
00:09:20.717 --> 00:09:22.351
Nikola: Your configuration has upgraded.

151
00:09:22.351 --> 00:09:24.802
Nikola: The running process hasn't agreed yet.

152
00:09:25.042 --> 00:09:25.751
Tyler: Fair point.

153
00:09:25.751 --> 00:09:28.235
Tyler: What evidence gets the process a vote?

154
00:09:28.475 --> 00:09:30.693
Nikola: Observe the running agent version.

155
00:09:30.693 --> 00:09:35.129
Nikola: Where relevant, identify the SDK bundled inside that agent too.

156
00:09:35.129 --> 00:09:38.678
Nikola: Those are related version numbers, not interchangeable labels.

157
00:09:38.918 --> 00:09:41.745
Tyler: And compare the application image identity.

158
00:09:41.745 --> 00:09:50.225
Tyler: The intended result is new instrumentation with the same application image, while acknowledging any replacement pods or restart.

159
00:09:50.465 --> 00:09:56.582
Nikola: Then send a fresh request and check the expected method span, parent relationships, and baseline signals.

160
00:09:56.582 --> 00:10:00.404
Nikola: Starting successfully isn't the same as exporting the right telemetry.

161
00:10:00.644 --> 00:10:08.809
Tyler: So this complete comparison answers three questions: what did we request, what is actually running, and did we rebuild the application?

162
00:10:09.049 --> 00:10:09.359
Nikola: Yes.

163
00:10:09.359 --> 00:10:13.080
Nikola: A single version field would leave at least two of those open.

164
00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:18.660
Nikola: And record the rollback path as part of the upgrade, rather than discovering it when a candidate fails.

165
00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:19.299
Tyler: Good.

166
00:10:19.299 --> 00:10:24.480
Tyler: Let's see whether that same lifecycle can take the extra detail away again.

167
00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:31.131
Nikola: The investigation ends, so selected detail needs a way out as well as a way in.

168
00:10:31.131 --> 00:10:34.214
Nikola: Retain it deliberately, or return to the breadth configuration.

169
00:10:34.454 --> 00:10:36.458
Tyler: We're removing the method span.

170
00:10:36.458 --> 00:10:40.066
Tyler: Unfortunately, the pricing calculation doesn't go away with it.

171
00:10:40.306 --> 00:10:42.777
Nikola: If only performance fixes were that easy.

172
00:10:42.777 --> 00:10:47.014
Nikola: The application still does the work; we're changing the observations it emits.

173
00:10:47.254 --> 00:10:51.848
Tyler: With startup agent injection, removal can require replacement processes too.

174
00:10:51.848 --> 00:10:58.279
Tyler: We request breadth, follow convergence, and keep the earlier depth request available for comparison.

175
00:10:58.519 --> 00:11:01.075
Nikola: Now inspect the illustrated fresh baseline request.

176
00:11:01.075 --> 00:11:05.456
Nikola: The selected method span is absent, while the service path remains connected.

177
00:11:05.696 --> 00:11:09.322
Tyler: But one missing span doesn't prove the baseline survived.

178
00:11:09.322 --> 00:11:13.755
Tyler: We also need the expected request relationships and the baseline metrics.

179
00:11:13.995 --> 00:11:17.090
Nikola: And an old trace can't establish a new configuration.

180
00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:21.560
Nikola: Use a fresh request from the converged workload and verify what actually arrived.

181
00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:24.929
Tyler: That's the reversible part of the operating model.

182
00:11:24.929 --> 00:11:31.579
Tyler: Depth can follow an investigation instead of becoming a permanent collection of every setting we've ever enabled.

183
00:11:31.819 --> 00:11:37.240
Nikola: Which raises the next question: what does that detail cost, and what information makes it worth keeping?

184
00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:39.472
Nikola: Tyler, this is where the units matter.

185
00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:43.768
Tyler: Instrumentation cost hides more than one question.

186
00:11:43.768 --> 00:11:46.441
Tyler: How much telemetry leaves the system?

187
00:11:46.441 --> 00:11:51.341
Tyler: And how much application resource use is associated with producing it?

188
00:11:51.581 --> 00:11:55.318
Nikola: I was hoping to count the spans, announce a saving, and finish early.

189
00:11:55.558 --> 00:11:56.022
Tyler: Tempting.

190
00:11:56.022 --> 00:11:58.344
Tyler: But which saving, measured where?

191
00:11:58.344 --> 00:12:02.523
Tyler: Span count alone doesn't answer either of those questions.

192
00:12:02.763 --> 00:12:04.773
Nikola: Let's start with export volume.

193
00:12:04.773 --> 00:12:06.784
Nikola: What comparison would be useful?

194
00:12:07.024 --> 00:12:11.635
Tyler: OTLP bytes per completed request, at a stated measurement boundary.

195
00:12:11.635 --> 00:12:15.323
Tyler: Keep breadth, selected depth, and restored breadth together.

196
00:12:15.323 --> 00:12:21.316
Tyler: Otherwise a smaller total might just mean that one run completed less work.

197
00:12:21.556 --> 00:12:24.158
Nikola: The denominator gets a speaking part.

198
00:12:24.158 --> 00:12:24.592
Nikola: Good.

199
00:12:24.592 --> 00:12:26.760
Nikola: And the application CPU view?

200
00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:30.563
Tyler: Compare CPU use at matched completed-request throughput.

201
00:12:30.563 --> 00:12:36.672
Tyler: State the unit and measurement location, and say which components are included.

202
00:12:36.672 --> 00:12:42.781
Tyler: Application CPU doesn't automatically include the node agent, Collector, network, or backend.

203
00:12:43.021 --> 00:12:50.969
Nikola: So we can't turn a change at one layer into a claim about the entire system, or translate a span count directly into a bill.

204
00:12:51.209 --> 00:12:58.790
Tyler: We also need comparable conditions: workload, request mix, sampling, warm-up treatment, and repeated trials.

205
00:12:58.790 --> 00:12:59.873
Tyler: Report variation.

206
00:12:59.873 --> 00:13:07.996
Tyler: If a configuration fails requests or slows completion, discuss that before interpreting a normalized number.

207
00:13:08.236 --> 00:13:12.806
Nikola: Otherwise the cheapest profile might be the one that stopped doing the job.

208
00:13:12.806 --> 00:13:14.563
Nikola: That's a fairly important footnote.

209
00:13:14.803 --> 00:13:15.273
Tyler: Exactly.

210
00:13:15.273 --> 00:13:19.505
Tyler: Correctness and completed work belong in the measurement context.

211
00:13:19.505 --> 00:13:23.267
Tyler: Cost without those can be a misleading comparison.

212
00:13:23.507 --> 00:13:26.724
Nikola: What do the empty plotting areas mean here?

213
00:13:26.964 --> 00:13:29.086
Tyler: The measurements are still pending.

214
00:13:29.086 --> 00:13:33.330
Tyler: There is no percentage saving to report from this illustration.

215
00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:40.968
Tyler: A small or inconclusive difference would still help us decide, or show where the experiment needs more precision.

216
00:13:41.208 --> 00:13:47.708
Nikola: Then the decision is specific to the service and investigation: did the information we gained justify the effect we measured?

217
00:13:47.948 --> 00:13:50.563
Tyler: Keep detail when it earns its place.

218
00:13:50.563 --> 00:13:53.179
Tyler: Remove it when it no longer helps.

219
00:13:53.179 --> 00:13:59.531
Tyler: And don't promise a faster application or a smaller bill just because a graph has fewer bars.

220
00:14:00.650 --> 00:14:04.702
Tyler: There's one more gate before relying on a profile change.

221
00:14:04.702 --> 00:14:09.159
Tyler: A workload can accept requests while its telemetry is still wrong.

222
00:14:09.399 --> 00:14:10.888
Nikola: The pod is green.

223
00:14:10.888 --> 00:14:13.865
Nikola: You're saying the trace needs its own interview?

224
00:14:14.105 --> 00:14:15.561
Tyler: A short one.

225
00:14:15.561 --> 00:14:23.813
Tyler: A successful HTTP response doesn't establish the parent chain, the intended span producer, or the baseline signals.

226
00:14:23.813 --> 00:14:26.725
Tyler: Application readiness answers a different question.

227
00:14:26.965 --> 00:14:29.023
Nikola: Then put verification before promotion.

228
00:14:29.023 --> 00:14:37.251
Nikola: Inspect a fresh request for the expected relationships, selected method detail where required, and the baseline signals that should remain.

229
00:14:37.491 --> 00:14:43.841
Tyler: Check observed configuration and version when they're part of the change too.

230
00:14:43.841 --> 00:14:45.958
Tyler: Desired settings describe intent.

231
00:14:45.958 --> 00:14:48.604
Tyler: Exported telemetry helps establish behavior.

232
00:14:48.844 --> 00:14:52.521
Nikola: How does that affect a rollout while old requests are still in flight?

233
00:14:52.761 --> 00:14:59.099
Tyler: A candidate can stay away from normal traffic until it passes the relevant checks.

234
00:14:59.099 --> 00:15:05.890
Tyler: The previous process may need time to finish its requests and export before it retires.

235
00:15:05.890 --> 00:15:09.965
Tyler: Candidate checks, routing, and export drain are separate responsibilities.

236
00:15:10.205 --> 00:15:13.093
Nikola: And those boundaries depend on the actual mechanism.

237
00:15:13.093 --> 00:15:17.787
Nikola: They aren't all guaranteed by a ready indicator or a released Operator feature.

238
00:15:18.027 --> 00:15:22.587
Tyler: If the checks pass, promote and retire according to that mechanism.

239
00:15:22.587 --> 00:15:27.562
Tyler: If they fail, preserve a usable state and follow the recovery path.

240
00:15:27.562 --> 00:15:33.366
Tyler: A rejected candidate should be something we can explain, not an unexpected plot twist.

241
00:15:33.606 --> 00:15:39.332
Nikola: We should exercise the path between steady states, including rejection and return to breadth.

242
00:15:39.332 --> 00:15:42.195
Nikola: Two attractive screenshots don't establish the transition.

243
00:15:42.435 --> 00:15:46.975
Tyler: And define the load and request patterns under which those checks apply.

244
00:15:46.975 --> 00:15:50.380
Tyler: That gives the claim a scope people can assess.

245
00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:52.311
Nikola: Candidate, verify, promote, retire.

246
00:15:52.311 --> 00:15:59.498
Nikola: We can use that order to reason about a change even when our platforms use different controllers.

247
00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:02.848
Nikola: Let's make this something people can try.

248
00:16:02.848 --> 00:16:06.616
Nikola: Pick one supported service and a question that matters to its owner.

249
00:16:06.856 --> 00:16:11.225
Tyler: Not instrument the entire fleet and hope a question turns up?

250
00:16:11.465 --> 00:16:14.456
Nikola: Let's give the fleet a quiet afternoon.

251
00:16:14.456 --> 00:16:16.166
Nikola: First establish baseline telemetry.

252
00:16:16.166 --> 00:16:20.439
Nikola: Ask whether that existing view can already answer the question.

253
00:16:20.679 --> 00:16:24.924
Tyler: If it can, we have useful evidence without changing another profile.

254
00:16:24.924 --> 00:16:29.554
Tyler: If it can't, what would the next observation need to tell us?

255
00:16:29.794 --> 00:16:32.573
Nikola: Choose detail that could change the diagnosis.

256
00:16:32.573 --> 00:16:35.351
Nikola: Work with someone who understands the application.

257
00:16:35.351 --> 00:16:42.894
Nikola: For a named method, knowing which operation to select is part of the investigation, not an automatic discovery feature.

258
00:16:43.134 --> 00:16:53.106
Tyler: Then apply the profile through the chosen mechanism, compare a fresh request, and check the relationships, ownership, and application identity.

259
00:16:53.346 --> 00:16:55.945
Nikola: Measure the relevant cost under comparable load.

260
00:16:55.945 --> 00:17:01.144
Nikola: Keep the original question beside the result: did this observation actually help answer it?

261
00:17:01.384 --> 00:17:06.900
Tyler: That makes it harder to confuse having more telemetry with having a better answer.

262
00:17:07.140 --> 00:17:12.427
Nikola: Decide what to retain, or remove the selected detail and verify the baseline.

263
00:17:12.427 --> 00:17:15.680
Nikola: Keep a rollback path for the transition itself.

264
00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:20.154
Nikola: That makes this a bounded experiment an application team can understand.

265
00:17:20.394 --> 00:17:24.820
Tyler: What should we leave for the next person who wants to repeat it?

266
00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:33.561
Nikola: Prerequisites, supported conditions, pinned versions, each change operation, the observations that establish success, and the recovery procedure.

267
00:17:33.561 --> 00:17:38.062
Nikola: Enough to understand what enrolling a service will do.

268
00:17:38.302 --> 00:17:42.040
Tyler: One service, one question, and a clear way back.

269
00:17:42.040 --> 00:17:49.517
Tyler: That sounds like an experiment we can discuss, rather than a platform migration hidden in a debugging session.

270
00:17:50.650 --> 00:17:52.434
Nikola: Let's test that rule.

271
00:17:52.434 --> 00:17:54.663
Nikola: Which service is returning errors?

272
00:17:54.663 --> 00:18:00.014
Nikola: Do you inspect existing request signals first, or add method instrumentation first?

273
00:18:00.014 --> 00:18:02.243
Nikola: Take five seconds to choose.

274
00:18:07.483 --> 00:18:10.307
Tyler: Start with the existing request signals.

275
00:18:10.307 --> 00:18:15.483
Tyler: They may already identify the service and the affected request path.

276
00:18:15.723 --> 00:18:16.173
Nikola: Correct.

277
00:18:16.173 --> 00:18:21.566
Nikola: Now change the question: which local calculation inside checkout consumes the time?

278
00:18:21.806 --> 00:18:25.250
Tyler: The baseline may leave that work unnamed.

279
00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:32.139
Tyler: Now selected runtime detail is worth considering, because the question needs a different observation.

280
00:18:32.379 --> 00:18:35.381
Nikola: Same system, different question, different next step.

281
00:18:35.381 --> 00:18:44.385
Nikola: These are hypothetical cases, but the decision rule travels: examine the evidence you have, then add what the unanswered question needs.

282
00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:46.969
Tyler: Back to checkout.

283
00:18:46.969 --> 00:18:51.805
Tyler: We started with a slow service and an unexplained local interval.

284
00:18:51.805 --> 00:18:59.279
Tyler: In the illustrated depth view, the pricing operation has a name and a place inside the request.

285
00:18:59.519 --> 00:19:01.802
Nikola: And checkout is still slow.

286
00:19:01.802 --> 00:19:06.826
Nikola: We should probably stop congratulating the tracing and investigate the calculation.

287
00:19:07.066 --> 00:19:08.666
Tyler: That's the point.

288
00:19:08.666 --> 00:19:14.532
Tyler: The explanation improved, so we have a specific operation to investigate.

289
00:19:14.532 --> 00:19:19.865
Tyler: Broad coverage got us oriented; selected detail closed a gap.

290
00:19:20.105 --> 00:19:24.602
Nikola: A shared deployment workflow should make those views manageable.

291
00:19:24.602 --> 00:19:32.097
Nikola: Upgrades, removal, measured cost, and verified transitions determine whether the workflow is useful to operate.

292
00:19:32.337 --> 00:19:34.569
Tyler: Your next step is small.

293
00:19:34.569 --> 00:19:35.908
Tyler: Choose one service.

294
00:19:35.908 --> 00:19:39.925
Tyler: Write down a question its current telemetry cannot answer.

295
00:19:39.925 --> 00:19:47.514
Tyler: Identify the observation that could close that gap, and what would convince you the change is correct.

296
00:19:47.754 --> 00:19:52.575
Nikola: Keep the detail that helps, and remove it when it no longer earns its place.

297
00:19:52.815 --> 00:19:53.806
Tyler: Start broad.

298
00:19:53.806 --> 00:19:55.789
Tyler: Add depth with purpose.

299
00:19:55.789 --> 00:19:58.267
Tyler: Manage and verify the profile.

300
00:19:58.267 --> 00:19:59.258
Tyler: Thank you.
